    Educational attainment was coded as either having not gone to college versus being a college graduate of any level, resulting in \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Education == "No College",])} participants with no college education and \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Education == "College Graduate",])} college graduates.
    Two participants had some college education, meaning they had not completed college.
    Both were ultimately collapsed in with college graduates under the assumption that one need not complete college in order for the educational system to have a potential impact on one's speech.

    A nearly omnipresent variable in variationist sociolinguistics is gender.
    Even more so than with socioeconomic class, the norm has been to code gender without explaining how this was done (\citeauthor[\foreign{e.g.},][]{sharma_cognitive_2011}, \citeyear[p.~411]{sharma_cognitive_2011}; \citeauthor{tagliamonte_subject_2023}, \citeyear[p.~307]{tagliamonte_subject_2023}; \citeauthor{travis_sydney_2024}, \citeyear{travis_sydney_2024}), presumably because it is taken for granted that one can identity a participant's gender without asking, although there are certainly exceptions to such assumptions \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][p.~39]{welker_syllable-final_2024}.
    In fact, sex is sometimes conflated with gender \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][p.~277]{roberts_what_2024}, whereas here I was specifically interested in the former rather than the latter.
    Participants were not directly asked their genders for the present study, however, as I did not want to risk creating social distance between myself and the participants who were residents of a politically conservative region of the US during a time when gender identity was a politically charged topic.
    Fortunately, French has grammatical gender as do even so-called basilectal forms of Creole to some extent \parencite[p.~170]{klingler_if_2003}.
    Gender was therefore coded based on how participants gendered themselves grammatically during interviews.
    This is admittedly imperfect as gender neutral forms such as the pronoun \lexi{iel} are not attested in Louisiana, making non-binary genders opaque in speakers' grammars, and those who are gender fluid would not be identified this way without multiple meetings that could elicit shifting genders.
    However, discussions did center on speakers' lives and identities, and some speakers did feel comfortable bringing up non-heteronormative identities, so it would not have been completely out of the question for them to also express transgender, non-binary or non-fixed gender identities where relevant.
    The final tally for gender then was \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Gender == "Woman",])} women and \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Gender == "Man",])} men divided relatively evenly between Creoles (\Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Gender == "Woman" & participants$Ethnicity == "Creole",])} women out of \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Creole",])} Creoles) and Cajuns (\Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Gender == "Woman" & participants$Ethnicity == "Cajun",])} women out of \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Cajun",])} Cajuns).

      Finally, the variable of racialized identity represents one of the most immediately important for the current study.
      While participants had their ethnicities coded as either Creole or Cajun ($N =$ \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Creole",])} and $N =$ \Sexpr{nrow(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Cajun",])}, respectively), racialized identity was meant to capture how they viewed themselves relative to the broader racialized categories of the US.
      Participants were asked to self-identify racially\footnote{
        This was not asked in the terminology of racialization as that is not how the community speaks about race.
      }, a question whose meaning generally needed no further elaboration or explanation.
      Despite a history of isolation from the broader US, participants are now thoroughly faced with its racialized social system.
      This does not mean that all participants gave categories familiar to any American such as White or Black as their racialized identities in all cases.
      Figure \ref{fig:race_counts} helps summarize the wide variety of choices that were made.
      These ranged from general American racialized categories (\foreign{e.g.}, White) to local categories (\foreign{e.g.}, Creole) to categories nuanced via combinations of categories (\foreign{e.g.}, Caucasian Cajun) to even the rejection of racialized labels (\foreign{i.e.}, transcendent\footnote{
        The value N/A represents the two participants for whom their racialized identity was not obtained, whereas the value transcendent came from a participant who explicitly rejected racialized labels.
      }).
      These were all initially coded as-is.

