  \section{Summary}
    The full range of information coded about participants can be seen in Table \ref{tab:participants_background}.
    All participants, in order to even be included in the study, were Creole or Cajun speakers of F\&C who resided in Lafayette Parish or one of the surrounding parishes at the time of the interviews, but other details about their lives were more or less variable.
    The sample of speakers skewed towards the older side and was almost entirely made up of those who had acquired F\&C naturalistically.
    On the other hand, there was some nuance given to self-identifications with racialized categories, and unsurprisingly, occupational histories were almost all unique.
    For quantitative analyses beyond descriptive statistics, some of these variables proved to be unusable, which will be discussed in the next two chapters.
    All of this information was relevant at one point or another at least qualitatively, however.